Yes, here is an example of how to automatically generate a table of contents (ToC) or an index of sections in a LaTeX document using the built-in tools in the popular LaTeX document class, LaTeX or LATEX (pronounced "lay-tek").

1. Begin your LaTeX document by adding the document class to the preamble. Here's an example:

   ```latex
   \documentclass{report}
   ```

   The `report` class is one of LaTeX's standard document classes that provides automatic generation of a table of contents. You can replace `report` with other document classes such as `book` or `article`, but note that their default behavior may differ in terms of additional features and configuration options.

2. After setting up the document class, add the following lines to tell LaTeX to include an automatically generated table of contents:

   ```latex
   \begin{document}
   \tableofcontents
   <contents of your document>
   \end{document}
   ```

   The `\begin{document}` and `\end{document}` commands delimit the "body" of your LaTeX document, and the `\tableofcontents` command inside it triggers the creation of the table of contents.

3. To include the sections in the table of contents, you must first define them in your LaTeX document. Assume that your sections are defined using the `\chapter`, `\section`, and `\subsection` commands, respectively. Here's an example:

   Assuming your document starts with the `report` class, the first section you might include would look like this:

   ```latex
   \documentclass{report}

   \begin{document}
     \tableofcontents

   \chapter{Introduction}

   \section{Chapter One}
   \subsection{Section 1.1}
   \subsection{Section 1.2}

   % the rest of your document
   \end{document}
   ```

   Note that the `\chapter` command is only available in the `report` and `book` classes, and the `\section`, `\subsection`, etc. Commands can be generalized using the `amsmectoc` package as follows:

   ```latex
