   \usepackage{amsmctoc}

   \begin{document}
     \tableofcontents

   \chapterintro{Introduction}

   \section{First Section}
   \subsection{Section 1.2}
   \subsubsection{First Subsection}

   % the rest of your document
   \end{document}
   ```

   Here, we've used `\documentclass` instead of `report` to keep this example more flexible, and `\usepackage{amsmctoc}` to include the package that provides generalized sectioning commands. The `\chapterintro` command is provided by the `amsmctoc` package and is used to create a chapter with a non-standard title (e.g., one that contains multiple lines).

   With the `\chapterintro{Introduction}` command above, the `amsmctoc` package will generate the following entry in the table of contents:

   `[Chapter 1] Introduction`

   If you use the `\section{First Section}` command, the `report` and `book` classes will generate the following entry by default:

   `[1.1] First Section`

   And if you use the `\section{First Section}` command from the `amsmctoc` package, you can also add subtitles to the section title using the `\subsubsection` command, which will be included in the table of contents as follows:

   `[1.1.1] First Subsection`

4. By default, LaTeX will automatically generate the table of contents at the beginning of your document, but it might not contain any content yet. To generate the table of contents correctly, you should follow these steps:

   a. Issue the `\tableofcontents` command at a location in your document that sets up the table of contents but allow some text to follow it. LaTeX usually won't output content at the beginning and end of the document, so this will ensure that the rest of your document still comes after the table of contents.

   b. Add the `\label` command right after the section you want to title. This will mark the section with a "target label" so that you can reference it later in the document.

   c. Add the contents of your document after the `\tableofcontents` command. This will allow LaTeX to generate the table of contents based on the sections that follow the command.

